const DF=["Gross pay is what you earn before cuts. Tax and other deductions come off, leaving take-home pay. Income can come from a salary, hourly wages, freelance fees or business profit, and it grows with skills, experience and the value you create.|Gross pay of 50,000 with 8,000 in deductions leaves 42,000 to spend, save and invest.|Budget from take-home pay, not gross. Relying on one income source is risky, so build a second one slowly.", "A budget is a plan for every unit of income. List income, then fixed costs (rent, bills), variable costs (food, travel) and savings. Compare plan with actual spending each month and adjust. The 50/30/20 rule gives 50% to needs, 30% to wants and 20% to saving or debt.|With 40,000 income: 20,000 needs, 12,000 wants, 8,000 savings.|A budget that is too strict fails. Leave a small fun allowance and review monthly.", "Pay yourself first: move money to savings the day income arrives, using an automatic transfer. Keep short-term savings somewhere safe and easy to reach, such as a savings account, and give each pot a purpose.|Saving 5,000 a month builds 60,000 in a year with no extra effort once it is automatic.|Saving what is left after spending usually means saving nothing. Save first, spend the rest.", "This is money kept only for shocks: job loss, medical bills or urgent repairs. Aim for three to six months of essential expenses in a safe account you can reach within a day or two.|If essentials cost 25,000 a month, target 75,000 to 150,000.|Start with one month and grow it. Do not put it in risky assets or spend it on sales and holidays.", "A credit report records your loans and repayment history, and a score summarises it. Lenders use it to decide approval and interest rate. On-time payment matters most, then low card use compared with your limit, a long history and few new applications.|Using 20,000 of a 100,000 limit is 20% utilization, which looks healthy.|One missed payment can lower your score. Check your report yearly for errors.", "Interest is the price of borrowing. A loan has a principal, a rate and a term, and each repayment covers interest plus some principal. Useful debt builds value (education, a home); harmful debt funds things that lose value at high rates.|Avalanche: pay the highest-rate debt first to save the most interest. Snowball: clear the smallest first for motivation.|Read the total repayment, fees and penalties before signing, not just the monthly amount.", "A card is a short-term loan. If you pay the full statement balance by the due date you usually pay no interest. Otherwise interest starts, often at a very high yearly rate, and minimum payments keep you in debt for years.|Spending 10,000 and paying only the minimum can cost far more than 10,000 in total.|Reward points are not worth paying interest. Avoid cash withdrawals on cards, as fees and interest start immediately.", "Investing puts money into assets that can grow: stocks (part ownership of companies), bonds (loans to governments or firms), mutual and index funds (baskets of assets) and real estate. Higher possible returns come with higher risk, so match choices to your goal and time horizon.|An index fund buys many companies at once, so one failure matters little.|Do not invest money you need within a few years in stocks, and avoid anyone promising guaranteed high returns.", "Interest earns interest. Each period, returns are added to the balance and the bigger balance earns more next time. Growth looks slow at first, then speeds up, so time matters more than the amount.|1,000 at 8% a year becomes about 2,159 in 10 years and about 10,063 in 30 years with no extra deposits.|It works against you on debt too. Starting five years earlier often beats saving more later.", "Risk is the chance that results differ from what you expect, including losses. Diversification spreads money across asset types, industries and countries so one bad result does not sink everything. Asset allocation is how you split it.|Holding stocks, bonds and some gold means a stock fall may be softened by the other assets.|Diversification reduces risk but never removes it. Do not put everything into one stock, even a famous one.", "Work out the yearly income you will need, then how many years you have to build it. Regular contributions to tax-advantaged retirement accounts, such as provident funds or pension schemes, grow through compounding and employer contributions.|Saving 10% of salary from age 25 builds a far bigger fund than 20% from age 40.|Avoid early withdrawals, and remember inflation: costs will be higher when you retire.", "Insurance moves a large, unlikely loss to an insurer in exchange for a regular premium. Health insurance covers hospital bills, term life insurance supports your family if you die, and vehicle and home insurance cover damage and theft.|A hospital stay costing 500,000 is paid by the insurer, while you paid only a small yearly premium.|Buy insurance for protection, not as an investment. Read exclusions, waiting periods and claim rules first.", "Governments collect tax on income, spending and property to fund services. Income tax is usually progressive: higher slices are taxed at higher rates. Deductions and exemptions reduce taxable income, and you must file by the deadline.|If income is 600,000 and eligible deductions are 100,000, tax is calculated on 500,000.|Keep receipts and records, file on time to avoid penalties, and check current rules because they change.", "Inflation is the general rise in prices. If prices rise 6% a year, 100 buys only about 94 worth of goods next year. Savings earning less than inflation lose buying power, so long-term goals need growth assets.|A snack costing 20 today costs about 36 in ten years at 6% inflation.|Compare your return with inflation (the real return) and avoid leaving large sums idle in cash for years.", "Needs come before wants. Compare total cost (price, delivery, running and repair costs), check reviews and warranty, and wait before big purchases. Sellers use urgency, anchoring and bundles to push you to spend.|A cheap printer with costly ink can cost more than a pricier one over two years.|Be wary of limited-time pressure and buy-now-pay-later schemes that hide interest and fees.", "Scammers copy banks, officials and companies, create urgency, and ask you to act fast: share an OTP, install an app or pay a fee. Common types are phishing messages, fake investment schemes, lottery and job scams, and UPI or OTP fraud.|A caller says your account will be blocked and asks for your OTP. This is a scam, because banks never ask for it.|Never share passwords, PINs or OTPs. Stop, verify through the official number, and report fraud quickly.", "Turn wishes into goals with an amount, a deadline and a monthly plan. Short-term goals (under 1 year), medium-term (1 to 5 years) and long-term (5+ years) need different places for your money: safe options for near goals, growth assets for far ones.|To save 120,000 for a laptop and a course in 2 years, put away 5,000 each month.|Write goals down, rank them, and review once a year as life changes."],DC=["Medieval music was mostly monophonic chant, one melody sung together. Over centuries, composers added extra voices (polyphony). Renaissance composers such as Palestrina wrote smooth, balanced vocal lines, and music printing from the early 1500s spread it widely.|Gregorian chant is a single melody. A Renaissance motet weaves four or more voices that enter one after another.|‘Historical’ often means Western art music, but music history is much wider than Europe. Historians now ask whose music is missing from the story.", "The Baroque era (about 1600–1750) used a bass line with chords and ornate melodies, with Bach and Handel. The Classical era (about 1750–1820) valued clarity and balanced form, with Haydn and Mozart. Romantic music (about 1820–1900) pushed emotion and imagination.|Listen to a Bach fugue, a Mozart sonata and a Chopin nocturne in a row. You can hear density, balance and expressiveness change.|Dates are approximate and styles overlap. Beethoven is often described as a bridge between Classical and Romantic.", "Historians study original manuscripts, early prints and letters. They compare sources, date paper and handwriting, and build critical editions that show where sources disagree. Staff notation grew from medieval methods such as those taught by Guido of Arezzo.|Two copies of the same piece may differ in a note or marking. The scholar decides which is likely correct and records the choice.|A printed score is an interpretation, not the composer’s only intention. Performers also rely on the style of the time.", "Ethnomusicology studies music in its cultural context. Researchers ask who makes the music, for whom, and for what purpose, such as weddings, work, worship or protest. An insider view (emic) and an outsider view (etic) are both used.|A harvest song is judged not only by its melody but by when it is sung, who sings and what it means to the community.|Avoid judging every tradition by Western standards. Terms like ‘primitive’ or ‘exotic’ are outdated and misleading.", "Fieldwork means spending time with musicians: recording, interviewing, taking part and learning to play. Many traditions pass on music orally, from teacher to student by ear and memory, rather than from a written score.|A researcher learns a folk drum rhythm by playing alongside a village drummer, then records and transcribes it with permission.|Recordings need consent and respect for ownership. Music belongs to people, not just to the archive.", "A raga is a melodic framework: a set of notes with rules for going up and down and characteristic phrases that evoke a mood. A tala is a repeating rhythmic cycle, such as Adi tala with eight beats. The Carnatic system organises 72 parent scales called melakarta ragas.|The seven notes are sa, ri, ga, ma, pa, dha and ni. A performer improvises within a raga’s rules while keeping time with the tala.|A raga is not just a scale. The same notes can feel different depending on emphasis, ornaments and phrases.", "Pitch depends on frequency, so concert A is 440 Hz and doubling the frequency gives the next octave. Loudness depends on amplitude. Timbre comes from the mix of harmonics, which is why a violin and a flute differ on the same note.|A guitar string vibrates as a whole and in halves, thirds and more at once. Those extra vibrations are the harmonics that colour its sound.|Loud sounds above about 85 decibels over long periods can damage hearing. Use ear protection at concerts.", "Listening involves the auditory cortex, motor areas that tap along and reward systems that release dopamine. Predictions matter: we enjoy music that balances expected and surprising events.|The ‘chills’ you feel at a favourite moment come with measurable body responses and reward-system activity.|The ‘Mozart effect’ is a myth. Listening to Mozart does not make you generally smarter, though music can help mood and focus.", "Sociomusicology looks at how music connects with class, community, age and power. Songs build group identity, mark rituals and carry protest. Technology and the market change who hears what.|A school anthem, a wedding song and a protest chant each bind a group together in a different way.|Taste is shaped by who we know and what is available. ‘Good music’ is never purely personal or purely objective.", "An octave has 12 semitones. The major scale follows the step pattern whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half and sounds bright. The natural minor scale follows whole, half, whole, whole, half, whole, whole and sounds darker. Modes such as Dorian start from other notes of a scale.|C major uses only the white keys on a piano from C to C. A minor uses the same white keys but starts on A.|Do not assume major means happy and minor means sad. Context, tempo and harmony matter too.", "A triad stacks a root, third and fifth. A major triad (C–E–G) sounds bright and a minor triad (C–E♭–G) sounds darker. Chords move in progressions, and the dominant chord (V) usually pulls toward home (I).|Many pop songs use I–V–vi–IV, such as C, G, A minor and F. The pattern feels familiar because we have heard it often.|Chord names describe sound, but many different voicings are possible. Analysis is a description of what happens, not a rule.", "Beat is the steady pulse and tempo is its speed in beats per minute. Metre groups beats, for example 4/4 or 3/4. Form is the overall plan, such as verse–chorus, AABA or sonata form (exposition, development, recapitulation).|A waltz is felt in three beats per bar, strong–weak–weak. Most pop songs use four.|Rhythm and metre are different. Syncopation puts emphasis against the expected beat, creating energy.", "Microphones turn sound into electrical signals that are recorded. Mixing balances levels and uses EQ, compression and reverb to shape each part. Mastering prepares the final stereo track so it sounds consistent everywhere.|A producer lowers a boomy bass, brightens a vocal with EQ and adds a short reverb to make a bedroom recording sound polished.|Louder is not better. Heavy compression to make tracks loud can remove the dynamics that make music feel alive.", "Digital audio samples the wave many times a second, and CD audio uses 44,100 samples per second to cover the range of human hearing. MIDI does not carry sound at all. It sends instructions such as note, velocity and length. A synthesizer builds sound from oscillators, filters and envelopes.|One MIDI performance can be played by a piano sound, a string sound or a synth, because it is only a list of instructions.|MIDI and audio are not the same thing. A MIDI file is tiny and editable, an audio file is a recording.", "Popular music studies look at genres, scenes, fans, lyrics and the music business. New tools can generate melodies, chords and drums from a text prompt, learning patterns from many recordings.|A user types ‘rainy night lo-fi beats’ and an AI system chooses a style, tempo and chords, then arranges and renders a short track.|AI music raises questions about copyright, originality and credit for the artists whose work shaped the models. Treat it as a tool, not a replacement for musicians."];
