var DEEP={"psychology/0.0":"Psychologists measure behaviour, thoughts and feelings, then test explanations with evidence. The field sits between biology and social science, so one question can be studied at the brain, mind or group level.|Why do we procrastinate? Biologically, stress and reward systems pull us to easy tasks. Cognitively, we underestimate how long work takes. Socially, friends and deadlines change what we do.|Psychology describes patterns across many people, not fixed rules for one person. Be careful with pop-psychology claims that have no evidence behind them.","psychology/0.1":"Wilhelm Wundt opened the first psychology lab in 1879. Later schools disagreed on what to study: inner experience, useful functions, observable behaviour, unconscious drives, personal growth or information processing. Modern psychology blends them.|Take a fear of dogs. A behaviourist looks at learned association, a cognitive psychologist at catastrophic thoughts, a psychoanalyst at hidden conflict and a biological psychologist at the brain’s alarm system.|Old theories matter for history, but not all of them survived testing. Ask what evidence supports an idea today.","psychology/0.2":"An experiment changes one factor and compares randomly assigned groups. A correlation shows two things move together, but it cannot prove one causes the other. Replication repeats a study to check the result holds.|Ice cream sales and sunburn both rise in summer. They are correlated, but the sun causes both, not the ice cream.|Small samples, leading questions and publication bias can mislead. The replication crisis showed some famous results were weaker than first reported.","psychology/1.0":"Information is encoded, held in short-term memory for seconds, and moved into long-term storage with attention and repetition. Short-term memory holds only a few chunks. Retrieval rebuilds memories rather than replaying them.|Spaced practice beats cramming. Review a topic after a day, three days and a week. Ebbinghaus’s forgetting curve showed we lose most new material quickly unless we revisit it.|Feeling sure is not the same as being right. Eyewitnesses can be confident and wrong, and leading questions can plant false details.","psychology/1.1":"Attention is selective: you focus on some signals and filter others, as when you hear your name across a noisy room. Perception combines incoming data with expectations from experience.|In a famous video, viewers count basketball passes and about half miss a person in a gorilla suit walking through. This is inattentional blindness.|Multitasking is really rapid task switching, and it costs accuracy. Using a phone while driving is far riskier than it feels.","psychology/1.2":"Kahneman describes System 1 as fast and automatic and System 2 as slow and effortful. Mental shortcuts called heuristics save effort but create biases such as anchoring, availability and confirmation bias.|A phone marked ‘was ₹15,000, now ₹10,000’ feels like a bargain because the first price anchors your judgement.|Knowing about biases does not remove them. Slow down for big decisions and actively look for evidence against your first idea.","psychology/2.0":"John Bowlby argued children need a secure base. Mary Ainsworth’s Strange Situation study described secure, anxious and avoidant patterns. Warm, responsive caregiving builds security.|A securely attached toddler explores freely, is upset when the parent leaves and calms quickly on their return.|Attachment styles are tendencies, not destiny. Later relationships and therapy can change them.","psychology/2.1":"Jean Piaget proposed four stages: sensorimotor (0–2 years), preoperational (2–7), concrete operational (7–11) and formal operational (12 and up). Each brings new abilities such as object permanence, logic and abstract thought.|In conservation tasks, the same water is poured into a tall thin glass. A five-year-old often says there is now more, while an eight-year-old knows the amount is unchanged.|Children often show skills earlier than Piaget predicted, and culture and teaching matter. Treat the stages as a rough map.","psychology/2.2":"Erik Erikson said each life stage has a central challenge, and for teens it is identity versus role confusion. The prefrontal cortex, which handles planning and impulse control, keeps maturing into the mid-twenties while reward systems are very active.|A teenager tries new friend groups, styles and ideas while working out who they are. That exploration is a normal part of building identity.|A developing brain is not a broken brain. Support, sleep and safe chances to try things matter more than labels.","psychology/3.0":"In Asch’s 1951 line study, about 75% of people went along with a wrong group answer at least once, and about a third of answers overall conformed. In Milgram’s 1963 study, 65% of participants gave the maximum 450 volt shock when an authority told them to.|Conformity fell sharply when even one other person disagreed with the group. Obedience fell when the authority was far away or lacked legitimacy.|Milgram’s methods are criticised on ethical grounds, such as deception and distress. The results do not mean people are helpless: an ally makes it much easier to say no.","psychology/3.1":"Leon Festinger’s cognitive dissonance theory says holding conflicting beliefs, or acting against a belief, is uncomfortable, so we change the belief, change the action or add excuses. Persuasion also uses credibility, emotion and social proof.|A smoker says ‘the risks are exaggerated’ to reduce dissonance. An advert saying ‘10,000 happy customers’ uses social proof.|Watch for urgency and scarcity tricks such as ‘only 2 left’. Ask what evidence supports the claim.","psychology/3.2":"John Darley and Bibb Latané found people help less when others are present, because responsibility is diffused and everyone watches others for cues about whether it is an emergency.|In a smoke-filled room study, people in groups reported the smoke less often than people who were alone.|Point to one person and give a clear request, such as ‘You in the blue shirt, please call for an ambulance.’ This breaks the spread of responsibility.","psychology/4.0":"A neuron receives signals, fires an electrical impulse along its axon and releases neurotransmitters across a synapse. The human brain has roughly 86 billion neurons. Dopamine relates to reward, serotonin to mood and sleep, and GABA to calming activity.|Learning strengthens connections between neurons. This ability to change with experience is called neuroplasticity.|The ‘left-brain, right-brain personality’ idea is a myth. Brain regions work together as networks.","psychology/4.1":"A threat triggers the fight-or-flight response: the amygdala raises the alarm, adrenaline and cortisol rise, and the heart speeds up. How you appraise the situation, as a threat or a challenge, changes the response.|Two students face the same exam. One sees a challenge and focuses, the other sees a threat and panics. Slow breathing helps calm the body’s alarm.|Short bursts of stress can help performance, but long-lasting stress harms sleep and health. If you feel overwhelmed, talk to someone you trust or a professional.","psychology/4.2":"A night is made of roughly 90-minute cycles through light, deep and REM sleep. Deep sleep supports physical recovery, and REM sleep supports memory and emotion. Light and regular timing set the circadian rhythm.|Teenagers naturally feel sleepy later at night, so very early school starts can leave them short of sleep. Bright screens at bedtime delay the body clock.|One long weekend sleep does not fully repay a week of sleep debt. A steady schedule works better.","psychology/5.0":"The Big Five are Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism (OCEAN). Each is a scale, most people sit in the middle, and traits change slowly over a lifetime.|High conscientiousness tends to go with reliable study habits. High neuroticism means a person is more easily stressed.|Popular type tests such as MBTI have weaker scientific support than the Big Five. Treat any test as a tendency, not a label.","psychology/5.1":"Behaviour followed by a reward tends to repeat, which is B. F. Skinner’s reinforcement. Pavlov showed that signals can become linked with responses. Self-determination theory says people thrive with autonomy, competence and connection.|A study app that gives a small reward after each session can build a habit. After weeks, simply sitting at your desk becomes the cue.|Too many outside rewards can reduce interest in things you already enjoy. Start with tiny habits you can repeat daily.","psychology/5.2":"Cognitive behavioural therapy links thoughts, feelings and behaviour. You notice unhelpful thoughts, test them against evidence and practise new behaviour. Psychologists assess and provide therapy, while psychiatrists are medical doctors who can prescribe medicine.|For exam anxiety, a person writes the fear (‘I will fail everything’), checks the evidence, breaks revision into small steps and practises relaxation.|This is general education, not a diagnosis. If you or someone you know is struggling, contact a doctor, a counsellor or a local helpline."};
