/* ===== v10: toys, plain-words articles, orbit look ===== */
PLAIN={
"finance/0":"Think of income as water flowing into a bucket. Your salary is the tap, and the size of the tap is gross pay, the number printed on your offer letter. But before the water reaches your bucket, taxes and deductions drink some on the way, so what lands is your take-home pay. That is the number you actually live on. Now picture three taps: a job, a side gig and an investment that pays you back. One tap can stop suddenly (layoffs happen), but three keep the bucket filling. A good first goal is not a bigger tap but a second one. If you earn ₹30,000 take-home and a weekend gig adds ₹6,000, you just gave yourself a 20% raise without asking anyone.",
"finance/1":"A budget is a plan that tells every rupee where to go before it wanders off. Imagine your salary arrives as 10 coins. The 50/30/20 rule says: put 5 coins in the NEEDS box (rent, food, transport), 3 in WANTS (movies, snacks, shopping) and 2 in the FUTURE box (savings and investing). When a box is empty, you stop spending from it. That is all budgeting is. It feels restrictive only when you do it by guessing, because then you find the money is gone on the 20th. With 40,000 take-home, that is 20,000 for needs, 12,000 for wants and 8,000 for the future, which is 96,000 saved in a year without feeling poor.",
"finance/8":"Compound interest is a snowball rolling downhill. A snowball starts small, and every turn it picks up snow, but here is the trick: the new snow also sticks and picks up more snow on the next turn. Money works the same way. You put in 1,000 and earn 8%, so you have 1,080. Next year you earn 8% on 1,080, not on 1,000, so you gain 86.4 instead of 80. The difference looks tiny, but after 30 years that snowball is 10,063 while a simple, non-snowball 8% would give only 3,400. Time is the hill. Starting at 22 instead of 32 can double your final amount, even if you save the same each month. A quick shortcut is the rule of 72: divide 72 by the rate and you get the years to double.",
"psychology/1.0":"Think of memory as a library with a very small front desk. New information lands on the desk first, which is short-term memory, and the desk only fits a few items for a few seconds. If you pay attention and repeat it, a librarian carries it into the shelves, long-term memory. Later, finding it again is retrieval, and the librarian rebuilds the story from clues rather than handing you an exact copy, which is why memories can change. Forgetting is mostly a failure to find the book, not the book disappearing. This is why spacing your revision works: each visit teaches the librarian exactly where to look.",
"psychology/3.0":"Imagine a quiet classroom where everyone points at a clearly shorter line and says it is the longest. You can see the truth, but saying something different feels awkward and risky. That pull to fit in is conformity, and in Asch’s study about three in four people gave a wrong answer at least once. Obedience is different: it is following an order from someone with authority, as in Milgram’s study where most people kept going when told to. The good news is that one ally breaks the spell. If a single person in the room disagrees, conformity drops sharply. So the practical lesson is simple: look for the other honest voice, or be that voice yourself.",
"musicology/6":"Sound is a vibration travelling through air, like ripples on a pond. How fast the ripples repeat is the frequency, and your ear hears that as pitch: fast ripples sound high, slow ones sound low. How tall the ripples are is the amplitude, which you hear as loudness. But two instruments playing the same note still sound different, because each note is really a stack of ripples at once, a main one plus higher ones called harmonics. The recipe of those extra ripples is timbre, the voice of an instrument. That is why a flute, a violin and a person singing can all hit 440 hertz and still be instantly recognisable.",
"musicology/9":"A scale is a staircase of notes, and the pattern of step sizes gives it a personality. On a piano, a half step is the smallest move, like going from one key to the very next, and a whole step is two of those. The major scale uses the steps whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half, which makes a bright, settled feeling. Keep the same notes but start from A on the white keys and you get natural minor, which feels darker. Try it in the Sound lab below: pick a root, choose Major, then Natural minor, press play and listen to how the same ladder shifts the mood. A pentatonic scale simply skips two steps, which is why it is so easy to improvise with.",
};
